<!--  The source-shared Plugin SDK (§4.4 of docs/vendor-plugins.md).

      This file is the ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the vendor-agnostic transport code a vendor plugin must compile
      into itself: the WMI COM layer (Windows), the sysfs/hwmon layer (Linux), the neutral port helpers, AND the
      C ABI envelope itself. Both the host and every vendor plugin <Import> it so the two sides cannot compile
      two copies of the same transport that then drift - the failure mode §4.4 rejects the duplicate-per-plugin
      option for.

      HOW A CONSUMER USES IT (this is P3's wiring, not P1's - the props is deliberately NOT imported by
      AcerHelper.csproj yet, so this slice does not touch the host csproj):

        <Import Project="..\..\Infrastructure\Plugins\Sdk\PluginSdk.props" />
        <ItemGroup>
          ... the same file-name OS split the host uses: a Compile Remove of **/*.Linux.cs on Windows and of
          **/*.Windows.cs on the portable TFM. The compiled path still ends in those suffixes, so a plain glob
          Remove selects correctly even though the items were declared here in the props.
        </ItemGroup>

      An EXPLICIT Compile list rather than a Compile Include="*.cs" glob: the folder holds only .cs today, but a
      glob is path-relative and would also sweep any obj/ (or a future generated file) that appears under it,
      silently compiling a build artefact into every plugin. The list is boring on purpose - a new SDK file
      fails loudly (it is simply not compiled) rather than quietly (an unexpected file IS).

      The Abi/ files are included here too: they are already dependency-free and the plugin needs them, so the
      host and the plugin share exactly one Abi.cs rather than each embedding its own copy (§3.1, §4.4). -->
<Project>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Transports and neutral port helpers. -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WmiSession.Windows.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WbemInterop.Windows.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WmiInvoker.Windows.cs" />
    <!-- GateStats is pulled in because WmiSession.Windows.cs (above) depends on it: the WMI COM layer times every
         EC transaction it serialises and feeds these always-on counters (Infrastructure/Diagnostics/GateStats.cs).
         It is vendor-agnostic, dependency-free (System.Diagnostics + Interlocked only) and portable, so it
         compiles into every plugin TFM; only the Windows half actually calls it, which is why it is listed beside
         the transport that uses it rather than as one more "Generic" file. It has NOT moved from Diagnostics, so
         the path reaches back there; the host still compiles its own copy (the same source-include-not-move rule
         as MachineInfo below). -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\Diagnostics\GateStats.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)SysfsInvoker.Linux.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)FirmwareAttributes.Linux.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Hwmon.Linux.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)SysfsLink.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)DelegatePorts.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)ProfileKind.cs" />

    <!-- MachineInfo is the ONE remaining vendor-agnostic Generic file the Acer code needs (docs/vendor-plugins.md
         §4.4, §3.2). The included Acer data/codec files carry a `using AcerHelper.Infrastructure.Vendors.Generic;`
         that is now DEAD in each of them (verified: no included file references a Generic member), but the C#
         compiler still requires the namespace to exist, and in a plugin it does not unless a type declares it.
         MachineInfo is the vendible, host-only-free member that supplies it: it reads the DMI manufacturer/product
         pair (`Win32_ComputerSystemProduct` on Windows, `/sys/class/dmi/id/{sys_vendor,product_name}` on Linux) —
         vendor-agnostic machine identity, the exact source §3.2 builds the `ah_matches` descriptor from. It is
         dependency-free in the plugin: MachineInfo.Windows.cs uses only WmiSession (already in this file) and
         MachineInfo.Linux.cs only System.IO.

         WHY LISTED HERE RATHER THAN MOVED. §4.4's rule is the SDK is the home for shared transports, but the task
         also forbids moving anything out of Vendors/Generic (the host still compiles it there). A source-include
         list is not a move: the host keeps compiling its own copy today, and when P4 imports this props into the
         host it must reconcile the duplicate exactly as it does for the transports it relocated to this folder.
         The paths reach back into Vendors/Generic because the props lives in Plugins/Sdk and the file has NOT
         moved; that is deliberate, so a future move is an obvious edit here and not a silent second copy. -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\Vendors\Generic\MachineInfo.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\Vendors\Generic\MachineInfo.Windows.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\Vendors\Generic\MachineInfo.Linux.cs" />

    <!-- The ABI envelope, shared verbatim with every plugin. -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Abi\VendorAbi.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Abi\AbiStatus.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Abi\Capability.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Abi\Operation.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
